Cause: `Command` is `#[serde(tag = "op")]`. serde's internally tagged visitor also has a `visit_seq` that takes the first element as the tag and the rest as the fields in order. The intents.json case "not an object" (`[1,2]`) is refused only because `1` is not a variant name. Paste into crates/taskdeck-core/tests/compile.rs. It fails at 7b36d27 on the first input (`expect_err` panics with `Ok(Forget { id: 5 })`):

// spec: CORE-INT-001
#[test]
fn an_array_is_not_an_intent() {
    for t in [r#"["forget",5]"#, r#"["rename",3,"n"]"#, r#"["quick_add","x",null]"#] {
        let e = parse_intent(t).expect_err(t);
        assert_eq!((e.code, e.status), ("core/intent_json", 400), "{t}");
    }
}

Run: cargo test --locked -p taskdeck-core --test compile an_array_is_not_an_intent. Impact is small: the array still has to match a variant's fields in order. But the parser accepts a second wire form that the spec refuses, and the page is treated as hostile. One possible fix: check that the parsed value is an object before the derive reads it, or read through a `serde_json::Value` first.

compile.rs:579 emits `base.unwrap_or(view.notes_rev)` with no range check. `LogCommand::to_canonical` (command.rs:167) turns the encoder's error into `unwrap_or_default()`, so the page's `{"op":"set_notes","text":"hi","base":9007199254740992}` gives `Ok(Some(SetNotes{base: 2^53}))`, which encodes to "". With base = u64::MAX the result is the same: empty bytes, and apply refuses it core/notes_stale. Paste into crates/taskdeck-core/tests/compile.rs. At 7b36d27 it fails with left: Err(CoreError { code: "core/not_json" }), right: Ok(SetNotes { text: "hi", base: 9007199254740992 }):

// spec: CORE-COMP-008
#[test]
fn set_notes_base_beyond_u53_is_not_emitted() {
    let view = BoardData::empty();
    let intent = parse_intent(r#"{"op":"set_notes","text":"hi","base":9007199254740992}"#).expect("parses");
    let overlay = Overlay::default();
    let cx = CompileCtx { view: &view, zone: &FixedZone(0), now: at(720), device_no: 1, overlay: &overlay };
    let mut minter = IdMinter::new(1);
    if let Ok(Compiled { command: Some(c), .. }) = compile(&intent, &cx, &mut minter) {
        let bytes = c.to_canonical();
        assert_eq!(parse_command(&bytes).as_ref(), Ok(&c), "{:?}", String::from_utf8_lossy(&bytes));
    }
}

Run: cargo test --locked -p taskdeck-core --test compile set_notes_base_beyond_u53. The engine would put an empty `cmd` into an entry, and every device refuses it at parse. set_notes is the only row that copies an unchecked page-supplied u64 into a command: every other id or sid must match something in `view` first. A related case, a base below 2^53 that differs from notes_rev, is already marked SPEC-QUESTION at compile.rs:578. See for_lead.
